module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.permissiveness_promoted_into_a_higher_safety_rung

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data permissiveness_promoted_into_a_higher_safety_rung: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "permissiveness_promoted_into_a_higher_safety_rung" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a refusal that is the FINAL policy is written up as a rung below a more capable one, and a next-rung trigger is filed promising to admit the case it deliberately refuses** (INVALID STATE: a class refuses some input; an author reasons that a future mechanism could HANDLE that input rather than refuse it, concludes the refusal is therefore below its ceiling, and files a DESIGN 4b(2) next-rung trigger naming the more capable mechanism. HARM: the trigger becomes a standing obligation to BREAK the semantics the refusal was chosen to protect, and it is discharged by making the system MORE PERMISSIVE, which moves the class DOWN the ladder while the roster records a climb. The row then reads as a known gap on the way to being closed, so the deliberate boundary loses its author and the next person to touch it satisfies the trigger in good faith. ",

    "**THE RECOGNITION RULE IS THAT THE TRIGGER'S DISCHARGE WIDENS THE ADMITTED SET.** Read the proposed trigger and ask what the system does AFTER it is satisfied that it does not do before. If the answer is 'accepts an input it currently refuses', the trigger is a capability request wearing a safety rung's clothes, and it belongs in a product decision rather than on the ladder. A genuine next-rung trigger does the opposite: after it is satisfied the same inputs are admitted and the same inputs refused, and what changed is that the refusal became unwritable rather than checked. THE LADDER MEASURES HOW A DECISION IS ENFORCED, NEVER HOW MANY CASES IT ADMITS. ",

    "**THE ORDER OF THE TWO QUESTIONS IS WHAT ACTUALLY GOES WRONG, AND IT IS AN ORDER ERROR RATHER THAN AN IGNORANCE ERROR.** 4b(2) says a class below its ceiling must name its next-rung trigger. That obligation is CONDITIONAL, and the condition is the part that gets skipped: the class must first be ESTABLISHED to sit below a ceiling. An author who feels the obligation before performing the derivation will always find a trigger, because a more capable mechanism can be imagined for any refusal. So the diligent-seeming move -- 'the rule says name a trigger, so I will name one' -- is exactly the move that manufactures the defect, and reviewers reward it because a row with a trigger looks more complete than a row without one. ",

    "**BOUNDARY AGAINST ceiling_never_exercised_for_a_population_the_census_cannot_plan AND AGAINST AN UNDERIVED CEILING, which are the two nearest neighbours and arrive from the opposite direction.** Asserting a ceiling NUMBER without deriving it claims the class cannot climb; this class assumes without deriving that the class CAN climb. Both skip the same derivation and they fail in opposite directions, which is why they are separate rows rather than one: the repair for an underived ceiling is to derive it and possibly climb, and the repair here is to derive it and possibly DELETE THE TRIGGER. A single row would make one of those two repairs invisible. ",

    "**RECEIPT, gunbc.scm.merge_base, 2026-09-06.** The squash merge refuses divergent per-path content at MANIFEST GRAIN rather than merging file contents line by line. This author wrote that absent line-level merging was 'a stated scope with a next-rung trigger: a text-difference capability'. The side-chat reviewer refused it: refusing divergent file contents can be the FINAL safety policy of the operation, and a text-difference capability increases ADMITTED MERGE POWER rather than safety. If the semantics are deliberately 'never synthesize file content neither author supplied', then text-level auto-merge VIOLATES those semantics instead of climbing toward them, and the trigger would have been an obligation to break the design. The trigger was not written; what was written instead is that manifest-grain conflict is a deliberate semantic boundary, with whether line-level merging is wanted AT ALL left open as a product question rather than presumed. ",

    "**A SECOND RECEIPT ON THE SAME LANE AND THE SAME DAY, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS A CLASS AND NOT AN ANECDOTE.** gunbc.scm.integration records the consumed source as a commit reference, which stops being a durable anchor if source commit records are ever PHYSICALLY DELETED rather than merely kept out of target ancestry. The reflex was to file that as a next-rung trigger. It is not one: retirement is a capability nobody has asked for, nothing establishes that this cut sits below a ceiling, and a trigger would have committed a future author to build durable provenance for a deletion feature that may never exist. It is recorded in that module as a FUTURE BOUNDARY -- what would have to be designed IF the capability is ever wanted -- which carries the same information and creates no obligation. THE REPAIR IS USUALLY A RESTATEMENT, NOT A DELETION: the knowledge is worth keeping, and only its status as a debt is false. ",

    "**CEILING: UNDERIVED, AND STATED SO RATHER THAN GUESSED, on this row's own rule.** Deciding mechanically whether a proposed trigger widens the admitted set requires comparing the semantics before and after a mechanism that does not exist yet, and this author has not established that comparison is decidable from modeled facts. Asserting a rung here would be the underived-ceiling error in a row about underived ceilings. What is honest today: the class is caught by review, it has been caught twice by one reviewer on one lane, and the instrument that found both was a question a person asked rather than anything executed. NEXT TRIGGER: none is named, because naming one would require knowing the ceiling. ",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
